    "created_at": "2024-10-16T01:43:21Z",
    "updated_at": "2024-10-16T01:43:21Z",
    "body": "Based on the recent trajectory of changes to this spec, it seems to me that the scope of the spec is client-side sniffing for cross-browser compatibility and protection for the user against malicious files. Sniffing plaintext into XML is a privilege escalation that I don't think the spec will ever abide. (Sniffing XML into a variant like SVG is a separate question that I don't think is being discussed in this context.)\n\nThe needs of a server are going to be dramatically different from a client's, and a server is going to have more detailed and reliable information at its disposal at file type determination time, and it is likely unrealistic for this spec to be comprehensive enough to meet the needs of a server's algorithm. It also seems less likely that such an algorithm would need to be standardized for interoperability. (The repository behind Linux's `file` is probably an example of a better fit for such a thing.)",
